  "description": "Learn how to talk about your family members, relatives, and loved ones in German.",
  "guidebook": "### Talking About Family\n\nTo talk about your family in German, you will use possessive words like **mein** (my) and **dein** (your). Notice how their endings change based on grammatical gender:\n\n- **Masculine & Neuter:** *mein Vater* (my father), *mein Kind* (my child)\n- **Feminine & Plural:** *meine Mutter* (my mother), *meine Eltern* (my parents)\n\n### Informal Grandparents\nWhile *Großmutter* and *Großvater* are standard, Germans almost always refer to their grandparents affectionately as **die Oma** (grandma) and **der Opa** (grandpa).\n\n### Using \"Haben\"\nWhen saying you have a family member, masculine words change in the accusative case: *Ich habe **einen** Bruder*, but *Ich habe **eine** Schwester*.",
